Traffic Shaping algorithms in C – the two classic ones are:
 Leaky Bucket algorithm
 Token Bucket algorithm
1.	Algorithm: Leaky Bucket
Input:
•	Bucket capacity B
•	Outgoing rate R
•	Number of time units N
•	Number of incoming packets in each time unit P[i]

Algorithm steps:
1.	Initialize:
o	Set stored = 0 // number of packets currently in bucket
2.	For each time unit i from 1 to N:
a. Read number of incoming packets P[i]
b. Check if stored + P[i] > B
o	If yes:
	Packet overflow = stored + P[i] - B
	Drop the overflow packets
	Set stored = B
o	Else:
	stored = stored + P[i]
c. If stored <= R:
o	Send all packets (stored)
o	stored = 0
d. Else:
o	Send R packets
o	stored = stored - R
e. Print number of packets sent and packets left in bucket
3.	After all time units:
o	While stored > 0:
	If stored <= R:
	Send all remaining packets
	stored = 0
	Else:
	Send R packets
	stored = stored - R
4.	End
•	Fixed output rate R
•	Excess incoming packets beyond capacity B are dropped
•	Smoothens bursty input to fixed output rate

 Leaky Bucket Algorithm (C Program)
This simulates a fixed-rate outflow of packets, buffering bursty input.
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int bucket_size, n, i, incoming, outgoing, stored = 0;

    printf("Leaky Bucket Algorithm Simulation\n");
    printf("Enter bucket size (capacity): ");
    scanf("%d", &bucket_size);
    printf("Enter outgoing rate: ");
    scanf("%d", &outgoing);
    printf("Enter number of packets (time units): ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    for(i=0; i<n; i++) {
        printf("\nTime unit %d:\n", i+1);
        printf("Enter number of incoming packets: ");
        scanf("%d", &incoming);

        if(incoming + stored > bucket_size) {
            printf("Bucket overflow! Packet loss = %d\n", incoming + stored - bucket_size);
            stored = bucket_size;
        } else {
            stored += incoming;
        }

        if(stored <= outgoing) {
            printf("Packets sent: %d\n", stored);
            stored = 0;
        } else {
            printf("Packets sent: %d\n", outgoing);
            stored -= outgoing;
        }

        printf("Packets remaining in bucket: %d\n", stored);
    }

    // Drain remaining packets
    while(stored > 0) {
        printf("\nDraining remaining packets...\n");
        if(stored <= outgoing) {
            printf("Packets sent: %d\n", stored);
            stored = 0;
        } else {
            printf("Packets sent: %d\n", outgoing);
            stored -= outgoing;
        }
        printf("Packets left in bucket: %d\n", stored);
    }

    printf("\nSimulation complete.\n");
    return 0;
}
2.	Algorithm: Token Bucket
Input:
•	Bucket capacity B (max tokens)
•	Token generation rate R (tokens added per time unit)
•	Number of time units N
•	Number of packets to send in each time unit P[i]
 
Algorithm steps:
1.	Initialize:
o	Set tokens = 0 // current number of tokens
2.	For each time unit i from 1 to N:
a. Add R tokens: tokens = tokens + R
b. If tokens > B:
o	tokens = B
c. Read number of packets to send P[i]
d. If tokens >= P[i]:
o	Send P[i] packets
o	tokens = tokens - P[i]
e. Else:
o	Send tokens packets
o	Packets dropped = P[i] - tokens
o	tokens = 0
f. Print packets sent, tokens left, and packets dropped
3.	End
•	Allows bursty traffic if enough tokens are available
•	Tokens accumulate up to capacity B
•	Packets sent depend on available tokens; excess packets are dropped

 Token Bucket Algorithm (C Program)
This allows burstiness controlled by tokens.
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int bucket_size, n, rate, tokens = 0, incoming, i;

    printf("Token Bucket Algorithm Simulation\n");
    printf("Enter bucket size (capacity): ");
    scanf("%d", &bucket_size);
    printf("Enter token generation rate: ");
    scanf("%d", &rate);
    printf("Enter number of packets (time units): ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    for(i=0; i<n; i++) {
        printf("\nTime unit %d:\n", i+1);

        tokens += rate;
        if(tokens > bucket_size) tokens = bucket_size;

        printf("Enter number of packets to send: ");
        scanf("%d", &incoming);

        if(tokens >= incoming) {
            printf("Packets sent: %d\n", incoming);
            tokens -= incoming;
        } else {
            printf("Only %d tokens available, packets sent: %d, packets dropped: %d\n",
                    tokens, tokens, incoming - tokens);
            tokens = 0;
        }

        printf("Tokens left: %d\n", tokens);
    }

    printf("\nSimulation complete.\n");
    return 0;
}
To compile & run (same for both):
gcc leaky_bucket.c -o leaky
./leaky
or
gcc token_bucket.c -o token
./token
Explanation Leaky bucket keeps fixed outflow, dropping excess.
•	Token bucket allows bursts as long as tokens are available.

